The '''relative pronoun''' is ''yo''. It is declined with the same endings as ''so'' (see above). 

The '''interrogative''' is ''ko'', which also takes the same endings, except that the neuter ("what") is ''kiṁ''.<ref name=":8">Perniola (1997: 55-56), Collins (2003: 67)</ref> From it are derived ''katara'', ''katama'' "which", ''kati'' "how many" and ''kittaka'' "how much".

An '''indefinite''' pronoun can be formed by adding the particle ''-ci'' to the forms of the interrogative: ''koci'' "someone" <ref name=":8" />

=== Verbal inflection ===
Pali verbs inflect for [[grammatical tense|tense]] (present, [[preterite tense|preterite]] and future), [[grammatical voice|voice]] (active and passive) and [[grammatical mood|mood]] (indicative, [[optative mood|optative]], [[conditional mood|conditional]] and [[imperative mood|imperative]]).<ref>Collins (2005: 79)</ref> They also agree with the subject in [[person (grammar)|person]] (1st, 2nd and 3rd) and [[grammatical number|number]] (singular and plural). The Old Indic [[middle voice|middle]] is preserved, but is rarely used<ref>Perniola (1997: 339)</ref>, survives only in some forms<ref>Oberlies (2001: 199-200)</ref> and there is rarely a semantic distinction between it and the corresponding active, so it may be described simply as a type of conjugation.<ref>Collins (2005: 78)</ref> When it does express a meaning, it is an action done for the benefit of the subject.<ref>Perniola (1997: 339)</ref>

==== Endings ====
There are three sets of endings:<ref>Perniola 1997: 71, 84</ref>
* primary: used in the present and the future (and sometimes in the optative);
* secondary: used in the preterite and the conditional (and sometimes in the optative);
* imperative. 

The endings are as follows:<ref>Perniola (1997: 71, 84)</ref><ref>Oberlies (2001: 217-218, 220-222, 226, 238-241)</ref>
